    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Have you been needing much less sleep than usual and still felt energized?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Less sleep? Man, I wish. I mean, no, no, not at all. I definitely don't feel energized. It's actually the exact opposite, which is probably the most frustrating part of this whole thing. \n\nBecause, you know, with the schedule we work—we do 24 on, 48 off, or sometimes you get mandated for a 48-hour shift if a guy calls out sick or is on vacation—sleep is already this precious, unpredictable commodity. You never know when the tones are gonna drop. It could be 2 AM for an elderly lift assist, or 4 AM for a dumpster fire, or you might be up all night at a multi-alarm blaze. So when you actually get a chance to sleep at the station, you're supposed to take it. And my body? My body is exhausted. I'm 37, carrying 70 pounds of turnout gear and air packs... I feel it in my knees, my lower back. I *want* to sleep. I know I desperately *need* the sleep. \n\nBut then I go into the bunk room, and it's like... I just lie there. I'm staring at the ceiling tiles, listening to the other guys snore, and my brain is just going and going and going. I'll be tossing and turning, thinking about, I don't know, fixing the busted latch on the compartment door on Engine 4, or realizing we need to restock the 4x4 gauze in the medical jump bag, or just completely random stuff that doesn't matter at all at three in the morning. I'm dead on my feet, completely drained, but I can't actually cross that bridge into falling asleep because I'm so keyed up. \n\nIt’s like... it's like your phone battery is at one percent, right? But the screen brightness is stuck on maximum and the flashlight won't turn off, and you can't plug it in. So, no, I definitely don't feel energized on less sleep. I feel totally wiped out. I just feel exhausted and wired at the exact same time, which is why I end up just getting out of bed and pacing the apparatus floor until the sun comes up.",
