    {
      "turn": 4,
      "doctor_question": "Were you like this as a kid too—especially having trouble paying attention in school?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Yeah, actually. It's funny you bring that up, because I was just talking to my mom about this whole situation with my captain, and she basically just laughed and said, \"Welcome to my world.\" She says I was exactly like this back in grade school, too. \n\nI mean, if you ask her, she’ll tell you I was an absolute nightmare for my teachers. I literally could not stay in my seat. You know those old school desks, the ones where the chair is connected to the desk part with that metal bar? I was always tipping them back, or I'd just stand up in the middle of a math lesson to go look out the window or sharpen my pencil for the fifth time that hour. I just had to be moving. And the blurting... man, I'd just blurt answers out all the time. The teacher would ask a question to the class, and before she even finished the sentence, the answer would just shoot out of my mouth. I wouldn't raise my hand, I wouldn't wait to be called on, it was just like this reflex I couldn't control. I wasn't trying to be a bad kid or disrespectful, I just couldn't hold it in.\n\nAnd the disorganization was just off the charts. My backpack was like a black hole. I was constantly losing my homework. The frustrating part was that half the time I’d actually sit at the kitchen table and do the worksheet, but then it would end up crumpled into a ball at the bottom of my bag under a pair of dirty gym socks, or I'd just leave it on the counter at home. So my mom was always getting notes sent home. Like, constantly. Those little handwritten notes from the teacher stapled to my agenda, or phone calls saying I was being disruptive or missing assignments again. \n\nWhich is the crazy part, because my mom always makes a point to say that I was bright enough to do the work. The teachers would even write that in the notes! They'd say, \"He's very smart, he catches on quickly, but he just won't apply himself.\" Like, I understood the material perfectly fine. If you asked me a question, I knew the answer—obviously, since I was shouting it across the room—but I just could not do the actual sitting still, focusing, and turning-in-the-paperwork part of school. So yeah, I guess it’s definitely not a new thing, it’s just... it's following me around, you know? Just playing out differently now that I'm an adult trying to keep my career from going off the rails.",
